Q: Is temporarily overriding equals method possible? A: <p><strong>Direct Answer to your question:</strong> it's not easily possible to &quot;override&quot; equals method in Java, However, there is way to achieve what you want. Here is what we can do:</p>
<p>User class:</p>
<pre><code>public class User {
    public String name;
    public int age;
    public int id;

    public User(String name, int age, int id) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        this.id = id;
    }
    public void check(int anotherId, String anotherName) {
        if(id == anotherId) {
            System.out.println(name + &quot;'s ID and &quot; + anotherName + &quot;'s IDs are equal.&quot;);
        }else {
            System.out.println(name + &quot;'s ID and &quot; + anotherName + &quot;'s IDs are NOT equal.&quot;);
        }
    }
    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>and Main class:</p>
<pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class Main {
    static List&lt;User&gt; users = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
    static String names;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        User user1 = new User(&quot;Bob&quot;, 19, 12345);
        User user2 = new User(&quot;Alex&quot;, 17, 12345);

        user1.check(user2.id, user2.name); //their IDs are equal.

        //Adding two users with identical name into List of Users.
        users.add(new User(&quot;Mia&quot;, 14, 3385));
        users.add(new User(&quot;Nick&quot;, 16, 125));
        users.add(new User(&quot;Mia&quot;, 20, 115));
        users.add(new User(&quot;George&quot;, 35, 342));

        //Checking if any Users have identical names.
        Map&lt;String, User&gt; seenUsers = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
        for (User user : users) {
            String name = user.getName();
            if (seenUsers.containsKey(name)) {
                User firstUser = seenUsers.get(name);
                System.out.println(&quot;The User (&quot; + firstUser.getName() + &quot;, Age: &quot; + firstUser.age + &quot;) and (&quot; + user.getName() + &quot;, Age: &quot; + user.age + &quot;) have the same names&quot;);
            } else {
                seenUsers.put(name, user);
            }
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>What we did:</strong><br />
- Created class User with its overloaded constructor that has parameters of name, age, id<br />
- added <code>check()</code> method to compare current User's name and another User's name manually.<br />
- Added <code>getName()</code> method with return type of String.<br />
- In Main class, I created two simple User Objects to compare their IDs and then I made a list with different names. but I added two users with the same names. and by using HashMap, for loop, and if-statement with in-built method of <code>containsKey()</code>, I checked if two users have the same names.</p>

